{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>These are the generations of Jacob.</b> These are the events of the generations of Jacob, their settlements and their unfoldings until they came to settled existence. The first cause — \"Joseph, being seventeen years old\" — through this they were driven onward and went down. This is the plain sense of the verse, so that the matter speaks for itself. This is the language of Rashi. But the expression \"generations\" does not properly apply to settlement. Rabbi Abraham said: these are the events that happened to him and the occurrences that befell him, in the sense of \"What will a day bring forth?\" But a man does not beget his own occurrences; rather, days are ascribed as begetting them. Perhaps it means \"these are the generations of the days of Jacob.\" But the correct interpretation in my eyes is: \"These are the generations of Jacob\" — Joseph and his brothers, whom he will mention, and Scripture employs a concise style with their names, since it has already mentioned them above. Rather, it says: these are the generations of Jacob — Joseph and his brothers, and what happened to them was as follows. It is also possible that \"these\" alludes to all those mentioned in this book: seventy souls went down, your fathers, to Egypt. For just as in \"these are the generations of Esau\" it mentioned sons and grandsons, kings and chiefs, all that were in it until the time of the Torah, so too it will enumerate in the generations of Jacob his sons and his sons' sons and all his seed; only, it will mention their details in their genealogies:" "<b>And he was a youth with the sons of Bilhah.</b> That he used to act in a youthful manner, adjusting his eyes and primping his hair. \"With the sons of Bilhah\" — meaning he associated with the sons of Bilhah, because his brothers would demean them and he would bring them close. \"The evil report of them\" — every evil thing he saw in his brothers, the sons of Leah, he would report to his father. This is the language of Rashi. But if so, why did the sons of the maidservants not save him, since he loved them and brought them close and reported to his father about the brothers who demeaned them? And if we say they feared the brothers — behold, they were four, and Reuben was with them, and Joseph himself, so their hand would prevail over them, even if the others would not engage in combat with them. Furthermore, it appears from Scripture that all of them agreed in selling him. But according to our Rabbis in Bereshit Rabbah: about all of them he would bring the report. The correct interpretation in my eyes is that this verse reverts to explain what it mentioned, and its ellipsis is: \"Joseph, and he was a youth, a son of seventeen years, was shepherding the flock with his brothers, with the sons of Bilhah and with the sons of Zilpah, the wives of his father.\" It is similar in this order (Genesis 40:5): \"And they both dreamed a dream, each man his dream in one night, each man according to the interpretation of his dream, the butler and the baker who belonged to the king of Egypt who were confined in the prison house\" — it reverts to explain the word \"they both\" that it mentioned, and its ellipsis is: \"And they both, the butler and the baker who belonged to the king of Egypt who were confined in the prison house, dreamed a dream, each man his dream in one night, each man according to the interpretation of his dream,\" and there are many such examples. Or \"and he\" may draw another word along with it, and its structure is: \"and he was a youth, and he was with the sons of Bilhah and with the sons of Zilpah, the wives of his father\" — it says that he was a youth and he was constantly with the sons of Bilhah and with the sons of Zilpah, the wives of his father, not separating from them because of his youthfulness, for their father had commanded them to watch over him and attend him, not the sons of the mistresses. And he would bring an evil report about them to their father, and therefore these four brothers hated him. Afterward it says that his father loved him, and his other brothers saw that their father loved him more than all of them and they were jealous of him and hated him. So he was hated by all of them: the sons of the mistresses were jealous of him — why should he love him more than them, when they are sons of a mistress just like him? And the sons of the maidservants, who would not be jealous over this matter of his being elevated above them, hated him because he was bringing their report to their father. The reason for \"their evil report\" is for emphasis, for every report is evil. And according to Rashi's opinion, it is possible that a report could be good, and \"one who brings a report\" is one who sees and tells, but \"one who utters a report is a fool\" — that is one who speaks falsehood. On the level of peshat, there is no difficulty that Scripture calls him a na'ar though he is seventeen years old, for because he was the youngest of all he is called thus, to say that he was not strong like his brothers and needed to be with the sons of Bilhah and Zilpah because of his youthfulness. Thus it is written concerning Rehoboam son of Solomon (2 Chronicles 13:7): \"And Rehoboam was young and tender-hearted and did not strengthen himself before them\" — and he was forty-one years old when he became king. And likewise \"Is it well with the young man, with Absalom?\" (2 Samuel 18:32), and Benjamin when he went down to Egypt was older than him, yet Scripture calls him na'ar many times. Onkelos translated \"and he was a youth\" as \"and he grew up with the sons of Bilhah\" — meaning that from the time he was a youth he was with them; they raised him like a father and they attended him. This too is correct, and according to the interpretation I have explained, Scripture relates that from these he would bring the report, and to the sons of the mistresses in their jealousy. The reason for \"the wives of his father\" is that they were wives whom he had taken as wives, and Scripture does not call them maidservants except when they are with Rachel and Leah, who are mistresses over them. Thus also \"And he placed the maidservants and their children\" (Genesis 33:2) — to say that because they were maidservants to Rachel and Leah he placed them before them. And likewise \"And he lay with Bilhah, his father's concubine\" (Genesis 35:22) — for if she had been a mistress, he would not have done so. And it is possible that during the lifetime of Rachel and Leah they are called maidservants and concubines, but now they had died and he had taken them as wives."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {0 "Ramban here summarizes and critiques Rashi's interpretation that 'these are the generations of Jacob' refers to the events of Jacob's life; he then cites Ibn Ezra ('Rabbi Abraham') and offers his own reading."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:41:19.380393083Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}